    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "When she was a child, Sandy Auburn’s father read her the comics in the local "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Akron Beacon Journal"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Slowly, she learnt to read them herself. As an adult, she reads the daily with her husband every morning over breakfast."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But now the newspaper is "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "turning her routine upside down",
            "ref": "v-routine"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". After over 100 years of "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "publication",
            "ref": "v-publication"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", it has stopped printing on Mondays and offers only a digital "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "edition",
            "zh": "电子版"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". “My first thought was, ‘Oh, my goodness’,” Auburn says. “This is a big change. The paper has been part of my daily life.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "For more than twenty years, researchers have been predicting the death of printed newspapers. Indeed, more and more publishers are "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "cutting back on",
            "ref": "v-cutback"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " print editions. Gannett is the largest newspaper publisher in the United States. It has stopped printing one day a week at nearly 200 of its papers. On these non-print days, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "subscribers",
            "zh": "订阅者"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " can download digital copies of the papers. Some newspapers have gone fully online."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many publishers point to rising newsprint costs. The publisher of the "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Dallas Morning News"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " said its newsprint costs had risen to $720 per ton from $520 two years ago. Some newspapers also argue that, as readers prefer the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "immediacy",
            "zh": "即时性"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and convenience of online news, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "print readership",
            "zh": "纸质版读者数量"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " has fallen in recent years."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Michael Sheare is an editor at the "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Akron Beacon Journal"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Since the paper stopped printing on Mondays, he has received many calls from subscribers. Though disappointed, most readers say they understand. Sheare has written articles to help readers get used to reading online. “The world has changed, and we have to change with it,” he says."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many younger readers have adapted. Michael Maghes, 46, now gets his news from apps. “When I’m on vacation, I’d love to read a printed paper,” he says. “But in the craziness of everyday life, I just "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "scroll",
            "zh": "滚屏"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " through the headlines. If it’s something I’m interested in, I’ll click on it.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As for 76-year-old Auburn, she and her husband still haven’t gotten used to online newspapers. They now save sections of Sunday’s "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Akron Beacon Journal"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to read the next day."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
